{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["Platt scaling","tree ensembles","calibration","discrimination"],"description":"The evidence from E1 focuses exclusively on isotonic calibration for tree ensemble models (random forest and naive Bayes), but does not evaluate Platt scaling, leaving a gap in understanding whether Platt scaling provides comparable or different improvements in calibration for tree ensembles. The comparison between Platt scaling and isotonic regression is absent.","evidence_ids":["E1"],"id":"G1","kind":"uncovered"},"hash":"9cd121358a9ac4481e44768119f5fe17361c91398790b12dd2a319d8ef49551a","kind":"artefact","prev":"3eb4ac5702308567f00dbc8fbf35032b5113cada6ce73b652ea8d11353f35032","seq":16,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T05:54:18.538+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["discrimination","tree ensembles","calibration","AUC"],"description":"The evidence from E2 evaluates calibration (ECE) and discriminative ability (AUC) for a Random Forest-based calibration method (ForeCal), but only on 43 UCI datasets and for DNNs, not for traditional tree ensembles. This limits generalizability to tree-based models like random forest and does not directly compare calibration performance across different tree ensemble models.","evidence_ids":["E2"],"id":"G2","kind":"narrow_scope"},"hash":"300a834b0f180194e4aab1aa9c159acb25e28af3d14fe4746a1a82243e8a0cb8","kind":"artefact","prev":"9cd121358a9ac4481e44768119f5fe17361c91398790b12dd2a319d8ef49551a","seq":17,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T05:54:18.538+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["discrimination","probability calibration","tree ensembles","AUC"],"description":"No evidence addresses the trade-off in discrimination (e.g., AUC, F1) under calibration for tree ensembles when using either Platt scaling or isotonic regression. While E2 mentions minimal impact on AUC, it does not provide data on how these methods affect discrimination across different classes or imbalanced datasets, which is critical for real-world tabular classification.","evidence_ids":["E2"],"id":"G3","kind":"uncovered"},"hash":"4bda1e28f2af4c84093b43c73ab9e457fb834b5b79844d1aead8ac9cc6ce8ce6","kind":"artefact","prev":"300a834b0f180194e4aab1aa9c159acb25e28af3d14fe4746a1a82243e8a0cb8","seq":18,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T05:54:18.538+00:00"}
{"actor":"director","data":{"action":"start","to":"hypothesis"},"hash":"7ac4fadd1b2d4c68724acf08752ec0e2aa158d31d172103625fafb9fa081b0f9","kind":"stage","prev":"4bda1e28f2af4c84093b43c73ab9e457fb834b5b79844d1aead8ac9cc6ce8ce6","seq":19,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T05:54:18.538+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"dependent_variable":"Expected Calibration Error (ECE)","evidence_ids":["E1"],"expected_direction":"decrease","gap_ids":["G1"],"id":"H1","independent_variable":"type of calibration method (Platt scaling vs. isotonic regression)","rationale":"E1 demonstrates that isotonic calibration significantly reduces ECE in tree ensembles (e.g., random forest), but does not evaluate Platt scaling. This hypothesis directly fills the gap by comparing both methods in the same model class (tree ensembles) and task (tabular classification), testing whether Platt scaling provides comparable improvements in calibration.","statement":"Post-hoc probability calibration using Platt scaling improves calibration (measured by Expected Calibration Error, ECE) in tree ensembles such as random forest on tabular classification datasets to a similar extent as isotonic regression."},"hash":"173ed0155a5853d279174e852068df633feb65bc2e62c10e0cfc6417263efc9a","kind":"artefact","prev":"7ac4fadd1b2d4c68724acf08752ec0e2aa158d31d172103625fafb9fa081b0f9","seq":20,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T05:54:18.538+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"dependent_variable":"AUC","evidence_ids":["E2"],"expected_direction":"no_difference","gap_ids":["G3"],"id":"H2","independent_variable":"type of calibration method (Platt scaling vs. isotonic regression)","rationale":"E2 reports minimal impact on AUC for a Random Forest-based calibration method (ForeCal), though applied to DNNs and not tree ensembles. This hypothesis extends that finding to tree ensembles and explicitly compares the two calibration methods, addressing the lack of data on discrimination trade-offs in tree ensembles under calibration.","statement":"Applying Platt scaling or isotonic regression to tree ensembles results in a negligible reduction in discriminative ability (measured by AUC) on tabular datasets, particularly in balanced and imbalanced classes."},"hash":"66615871876661d6b12eba6eafc36e30be6557e6d9aed80afe83e1645960203b","kind":"artefact","prev":"173ed0155a5853d279174e852068df633feb65bc2e62c10e0cfc6417263efc9a","seq":21,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T05:54:18.538+00:00"}
