{"actor":"critic","data":{"id":"C11","issue":"The design does not establish whether the control (random_forest) is truly a fair baseline for comparison with calibrated versions, as it does not specify how the calibration is applied to the model output (e.g., whether the original model probabilities are used or transformed). This introduces potential leakage if calibration is applied to the same data used for training, which would violate the principle of post-hoc calibration. The rationale assumes fair comparison but lacks explicit control over the calibration process's data usage.","message":"Lacks explicit control over calibration data usage, risking leakage.","resolution":"open","resolution_note":"","reviewer":"model","round":99,"severity":"minor","stage":"design","target_id":"D1"},"hash":"13865f3dee2fde3d4d9a4ba216dcd3531ceb9b8ec3847ed0cf61e7cddd0dace2","kind":"critique","prev":"749947b871ff1c145fcb1568fbef4e48d4f149a201a85cf5d990404faa15a1bb","seq":51,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T05:58:08.863+00:00"}
{"actor":"critic","data":{"id":"C12","issue":"The design includes imbalanced variants (e.g., 'imb10') but does not specify how the imbalance is defined or whether the class distribution is preserved across splits. This introduces ambiguity in the interpretation of AUC changes, especially since AUC is sensitive to class imbalance. Furthermore, the hypothesis claims a 'negligible reduction' in AUC without defining what constitutes 'negligible'—a threshold not provided in the design or evidence, making the claim unfalsifiable and unquantifiable.","message":"No definition of 'negligible' reduction; imbalance handling not specified; hypothesis not falsifiable.","resolution":"open","resolution_note":"","reviewer":"model","round":99,"severity":"major","stage":"design","target_id":"D2"},"hash":"2035a5a66b795677572bf9e24cd802183f4d86e9a713d40fcff2fb06a7d22f7a","kind":"critique","prev":"13865f3dee2fde3d4d9a4ba216dcd3531ceb9b8ec3847ed0cf61e7cddd0dace2","seq":52,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T05:58:08.863+00:00"}
{"actor":"critic","data":{"id":"C13","issue":"The hypothesis claims that Platt scaling and isotonic regression provide 'similar' improvements in ECE, but the evidence (E1) only shows that isotonic reduces ECE, without any data on Platt scaling. Thus, the hypothesis is not grounded in evidence and cannot be tested without additional data. It misrepresents the gap (G1) as being filled, when in fact the evidence does not support the comparison.","message":"Hypothesis not grounded in evidence; gap not supported by evidence.","resolution":"open","resolution_note":"","reviewer":"model","round":99,"severity":"major","stage":"design","target_id":"H1"},"hash":"930b18cde2f8ec4a3f7ea4b1e6ba1ac984ee9e013168ec4c36d04c19f8625d06","kind":"critique","prev":"2035a5a66b795677572bf9e24cd802183f4d86e9a713d40fcff2fb06a7d22f7a","seq":53,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T05:58:08.863+00:00"}
{"actor":"critic","data":{"id":"C14","issue":"The evidence (E2) is from a DNN-based method (ForeCal) and not from tree ensembles, so it does not support the claim about tree ensembles. The hypothesis directly extrapolates from non-applicable evidence, making it unsupported and invalid. The claim of 'negligible reduction' in AUC is not justified by any data on tree ensembles.","message":"Evidence not applicable to the model class; claim unsupported.","resolution":"open","resolution_note":"","reviewer":"model","round":99,"severity":"major","stage":"design","target_id":"H2"},"hash":"2a797728ccbc7979a43b16648c20c88eaa222b87f6e9a123a1460cfb4d809ccc","kind":"critique","prev":"930b18cde2f8ec4a3f7ea4b1e6ba1ac984ee9e013168ec4c36d04c19f8625d06","seq":54,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T05:58:08.863+00:00"}
